The issue is likely due to the fact that the `RequestValidationError` exception handler is not being triggered because the `Request` instance is not being accessed correctly in the exception handler. To fix this, you can use the `request` parameter in the exception handler to access the request body. For example, you can modify the `validation_exception_handler` to return the request body as follows:

```python
@app.exception_handler(RequestValidationError)
async def validation_exception_handler(request: Request, exc: RequestValidationError):
    return JSONResponse(
        status_code=422,
        content=jsonable_encoder({"detail": exc.errors(), "body": await request.body()}),
    )
```

This will return the request body as part of the error response, which should help you diagnose the issue.

To track the interruption point during large language model output streaming, you can use a custom `StreamingResponse` with `yield` to send each chunk of data in turn. This allows the client to cancel the stream at any point. You can also set the `Content-Type` header to `text/event-stream` to indicate that the response is a streaming event. 

Example:
```python
from fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse
class EventStreamingResponse(StreamingResponse):
    media_type = "text/event-stream"

@app.get("/stream")
async def stream(request: Request):
    async def event_generator():
        try:
            for i in range(100):  
                yield "data: Line {}\n\n".format(i)
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"Error occurred: {e}")

    return EventStreamingResponse(event_generator(), media_type="text/event-stream")
```
